<PageTitle>Environment reel guide · Lumibelle</PageTitle>
<article class="environment-reel-guide">
    <h1>Environment reference reels</h1>
    <p>Start with an image of your location and generate its missing viewpoints in one coherent reel. Save useful views as reference images or select keyframes for later shots, rather than generating each angle independently.</p>
    <p>In an image’s review/details dialog, choose <strong>Create reel from this image</strong> in the footer. This opens a fresh recipe with that image selected and preserves any earlier recipe. You can also choose <strong>Reference reel</strong> from the Assets tools’ <strong>Create</strong> selector.</p>
    <h2>Choose the coverage you need</h2>
    <p>These top-down diagrams illustrate camera travel and viewing direction, not a reconstruction of your location. Each preset completes its intended coverage within one reel.</p>
    <div class="coverage-examples">
        <section><EnvironmentReelCoverage Framing="ReelFraming.EnvironmentFullSet" /><p>Use for a room, courtyard or other acting area. The camera stays near the perimeter and looks inward, giving wide shooting positions and backgrounds from each side. Default: 15 seconds. Clockwise or counterclockwise is measured from above.</p></section>
        <section><EnvironmentReelCoverage Framing="ReelFraming.EnvironmentFeatureStudy" /><p>Use for a particular feature and its immediate surroundings. Name it in Instructions. The camera makes a complete orbit, with enough distance to keep the whole feature visible. This does not establish the entire set. Default: 15 seconds.</p></section>
        <section><EnvironmentReelCoverage Framing="ReelFraming.EnvironmentWalkthrough" /><p>Use for a street, corridor or connected rooms. Instructions must describe the route and destination, for example: “From the gate, along the arcade, to the courtroom door.” The drawing is an example; your route determines the coverage. Default: 15 seconds.</p></section>
        <section><EnvironmentReelCoverage Framing="ReelFraming.EnvironmentPath" /><p>Use for another coverage plan. Describe the starting view, route and destination, including any views to hold. Your instructions determine the movement and pace; the drawing is an example. Default: 10 seconds.</p></section>
    </div>
    <h2>Example: the Noxian plaza</h2>
    <p>A wide entrance image already shows the defendant’s podium, the raised judicial bench, a gate and the fortress skyline. For <strong>Full set</strong>, keep that broad scale and travel around the plaza’s edges looking across the acting area. Treat the podium as one landmark within the set, rather than approaching it or keeping it large in every view.</p>
    <p>For a <strong>Feature study</strong>, name “the defendant’s podium and its steps.” Orbit that smaller area to establish its other faces and immediate backgrounds. For a <strong>Walkthrough</strong>, name a connected route such as “from the gate, along the plaza’s edge, to the bench steps.”</p>
    <h2>References and Instructions</h2>
    <p>Select your clearest location image and any additional views of the same place. Crops, asset notes and reference guidance help define recognizable features and their physical relationships. The opening may use the source composition when it suits the route, or establish framing appropriate to the coverage.</p>
    <p>Full set can infer the acting area from your references and notes; name it in Instructions when needed. Feature study needs a named feature. Walkthrough needs a route and destination. Custom needs a starting view, route and destination. Review authored directions so they describe one coherent camera plan.</p>
    <p>Source images do not reveal every surface. Unseen areas are newly designed by the video model; additional references constrain them. All presets preserve stationary scenery and lighting, use clear camera routes and produce silent visual references.</p>
    <h2>Review and save the useful angles</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Review the composed prompt and use guidance, then generate the reel when ready.</li>
        <li>Watch the whole reel. Check that landmarks stay in the same physical places, doors and passages connect, and the camera reveals all intended sides without crossing scenery. A matching first and last frame alone does not establish consistent coverage.</li>
        <li>In <strong>Manage keyframes</strong>, choose <strong>Suggest views</strong> for AI to select complementary frames, name the visible viewpoints and flag uncertain coverage. Supply selection instructions if needed. Review or edit the names and notes, uncheck unwanted suggestions, then add them or replace your pending picks. <strong>Undo picks</strong> restores the previous selection; <strong>Save keyframes</strong> keeps it. <strong>More selection tools → Quick picks</strong> offers a fast local fallback without AI labels.</li>
        <li>Leave <strong>Views</strong> on <strong>Automatic</strong> for enough complementary coverage, up to nine views, or set a lower limit. Selection notes focus on suggested views and missing coverage; all inspected candidates remain in <strong>Request and response</strong>.</li>
        <li>Use those images or selected keyframes as shot references. Direct video references use only the reel’s beginning, up to the target shot’s length; a short shot will not receive all sides of a long reel that way.</li>
        <li>Correct use guidance to describe the environment the result actually establishes. Later shots choose their own camera movement, action and dialogue.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>View names describe actual visible landmarks, rather than assuming the generated reel followed its planned angles. Names and notes reach Compose in both <strong>Keyframes</strong> mode (individual numbered pictures) and <strong>RefMod</strong> mode (two to nine selected still views packed into one video-reference input, built automatically as needed). You can also export a selected frame as an independent asset image with its name and notes. Failed responses remain inspectable; choose <strong>Try again / change model</strong> to request another suggestion.</p>
    <p>The duration is requested time; generation uses the model’s frame grid. Shorter reels leave less time to travel and settle. Keep the intended area or route manageable within the 15-second limit, and check the resulting coverage before relying on it.</p>
    <h2>Compose from the environment library</h2>
    <p>In a shot setup’s References area, click <strong>Choose environment views</strong>, above <strong>Manage references</strong>. Choose an <strong>Environment</strong> asset in that dialog, then use <strong>Choose views with AI</strong> or <strong>Browse views</strong>. Its images and saved views across all its reels form the library. The environment choice and references are saved together when you apply; cancelling leaves the setup unchanged.</p>
    <p>The view assistant proposes useful viewpoints using the shot’s action, framing and included scene and neighbouring-shot context. Review thumbnails, source reels, timestamps and continuity concerns. Use <strong>Choose other views</strong> for a manual replacement. The budget includes retained character, prop and continuity references. Explicitly uncheck <strong>Keep</strong> to remove an older environment reference.</p>
    <p>View selection also receives the shot's current <strong>Scene strategy</strong> and <strong>Direction</strong>. It can adapt proposed positions and camera placement while preserving the dramatic purpose. <strong>Reference-supported staging</strong> links supported, inferred, missing or conflicting claims to captured pictures and crops. These are reviewable model assessments, not verified geometry. Changed direction, references or crops require review; Compose qualifies unsupported relationships instead of silently changing the story.</p>
    <p><strong>Apply views</strong> saves those references and closes the dialog. Finish selecting any other references in <strong>Manage references</strong>, then choose <strong>Compose prompt</strong> separately. Compose uses the attached views without selecting them again. If composition fails, the selected views and your previous prompt remain. Selected pictures are the default; RefMod is an explicit alternative requiring at least two selected frames from each reel. Missing media or unkeyframed reels are reported, with links to manage the sources. Large libraries are inspected in bounded batches, with no automatic extraction of extra frames.</p>
</article>
